  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 239 PM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nTuesday through midweek, the core of mid-level ridging will move \noverhead (flattening as it does), accompanied by high pressure at \nthe surface -- oriented such that we're likely to have an extended \nperiod of \"cold\" air damming. Heat will begin to build once again, \nwith highs returning to several degrees above average by mid-to-late \nweek. Breezy easterly winds should support ample mixing during the \nafternoon, which will initially serve to keep heat index values \nwithin a few degrees of air temperatures. Convective development \nwill be hampered by subsidence across the northern half of the \nforecast area, with scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms \npossible across south central Georgia through Wednesday. \n\nThe presiding mid-level ridge will retrograde westward across the \nFour Corners region on Thursday, allowing for more substantial \nmoisture intrusion across the Southeast (driven by southwesterly \nsurface winds), and a return to more uniform afternoon thunderstorm \nchances areawide. A cold front riding along the leading edge of \nshortwave troughing will sweep across the area over the course of \nthe day Friday, bolstering convective coverage and providing some \nsupport for at least quasi-organization into clusters. \n\nTuesday will be the \"coolest\" day in the long term (relatively \nspeaking), with highs in the 80s to near 90. Gradual warming will \noccur throughout the remainder of the period, with highs returning \nto the low-to-mid 90s for all but far northeast Georgia.",
